RESULT: PASS. Dangling-invoice detection is correct against real on-chain state, not just the
anvil-backed unit tests' synthetic setup.

================================================================================
PART 2 -- resumeFundResidualViaGateway live on Arc testnet + Ethereum Sepolia
================================================================================
Scripts: app/scripts/step13-resume-live-proof.mts, step13-resume-retry.mts (both deleted after use)

Deposited 1.30 USDC directly via `depositToGateway` (not through `fundResidualViaGateway`) --
deliberately simulating "the deposit already succeeded, now resume" without needing to wait for
another real transient failure to occur naturally, per the plan's own test scoping.

FIRST resumeFundResidualViaGateway call: while polling for confirmation, hit a REAL (not staged)
transient failure -- `NETWORK_GATEWAY_API_ERROR: Maximum retry attempts (10) exceeded: Request
timed out` from Circle's own `getConfirmedDeposits` endpoint, the same class of failure the
2026-09-20 Phase 2/3 campaign (step 12) hit twice already. This time, the function correctly
caught it and threw the new `GatewayFundResidualPartialFailureError` -- carrying sourceChain,
arcChainId, amountUsdc, depositAmountUsdc, and target verbatim -- rather than crashing with the
raw underlying error. This is real, unplanned confirmation that the wrapping behavior works
correctly even inside `resumeFundResidualViaGateway` itself (not just `fundResidualViaGateway`),
and that Circle's testnet Gateway API transient-failure rate is high enough that this project
should expect to hit it again, not treat it as a one-off.

RETRY (using the thrown error's own fields verbatim, per its own documented contract): resolved
successfully after ~4 minutes of polling:
  tx 0xa2346dc8e220838ca0033f584d19f4214b988277b545a115bb30242ece1755a6
  https://testnet.arcscan.app/tx/0xa2346dc8e220838ca0033f584d19f4214b988277b545a115bb30242ece1755a6

Independent on-chain verification:
  `cast receipt`: status 1 (success), `from` is the raw-key operator EOA's own address, a real
  `Transfer` log mints exactly 100,000 base units (0.10 USDC).
  Operator's Arc-testnet USDC balance: 46,819,693 -> 46,897,050 base units (+77,357, ~0.0774 USDC
  -- the same small forwarder-fee-split gap seen in every prior Unified Balance proof this
  project has run).
  Operator's Ethereum-Sepolia USDC balance: 11,700,000 -> 10,400,000 base units, a decrease of
  EXACTLY 1,300,000 (1.30 USDC) across the entire sequence -- the initial deposit, the failed
  resume attempt, and the successful retry combined. Proves `resumeFundResidualViaGateway` never
  deposited a second time, even across a real repeated transient failure, not just in the
  best-case single-call path.

RESULT: PASS -- and a stronger proof than originally planned, since the failure encountered was a
real one (Circle's own flaky testnet API), not a staged/simulated one.

================================================================================
PART 3 -- rpcUrl wiring smoke check
================================================================================
Script: app/scripts/step13-rpcurl-smoke.mts (deleted after use)
`createOperatorAdapter(privateKey, "https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com")` constructed
successfully and a real `getUsdcBalances` call through the resulting adapter succeeded. Confirms
the new optional `rpcUrl` parameter doesn't break adapter construction or basic calls. Not a full
proof that a *different, more reliable* RPC would have prevented the raw-key path's earlier
RPC_ENDPOINT_ERROR (this check reused the same public endpoint, since the point was structural,
not a reliability upgrade) -- the fix's value is making the endpoint configurable at all, which
this confirms works.

